issues.body:
The user-facing honesty note is rewritten to "Wall times are measured through the browser's performance.now() clock. Browser timer precision may be coarsened, so sub-resolution operators display '-'". No code in this diff measures anything.

Ground truth at the PR head, crates/sparq-engine/src/exec.rs:4840-4847 (the function that fills every operator's nanos via trace::exit):

  #[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]
  let start = std::time::Instant::now();
  let r = eval_graph_pattern_inner(graph, local, p);
  #[cfg(not(target_arch = "wasm32"))]
  let nanos = start.elapsed().as_nanos() as u64;
  // `Instant` is unusable on wasm32-unknown-unknown (it panics): rows only there.
  #[cfg(target_arch = "wasm32")]
  let nanos = 0u64;
  trace::exit(idx, r.as_ref().map_or(0, |b| b.rows.len()), nanos);

The wasm binding crates/sparq-wasm/src/explain_plan.rs:62 is a pass-through to sparq_engine::explain_plan_analyze and adds no clock. Repo-wide grep for performance.now / web-time / web_time / instant::Instant / js_sys::Date finds nothing in the explain path, and neither crates/sparq-wasm/Cargo.toml nor crates/sparq-engine/Cargo.toml carries a wasm clock dependency.

So EVERY wasm operator reads exactly 0 and renders "-" unconditionally. The new note tells the user that is a timer-resolution artifact ("sub-resolution"), implying their operators ran faster than the clock could resolve. Nothing was measured at all. This is strictly less honest than the text it replaces.
